{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecificLimits.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset"],"declarations":29,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.candidate_ratio_indep_of_anchor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.candidate_ratio_indep_of_anchor","kind":"theorem","line":327,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L327-L332","doc":"Every candidate scale has the shape `m · f(q)` with one dimensionful anchor\n`m`, so the ratio of two candidates is a pure function of `q`. A measurement of\n`m` alone cannot separate them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.residual_ratio_scale_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.residual_ratio_scale_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":320,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L320-L325","doc":"The ratio of the residuals `B/(E·S)` at two nuclides is the same at every\nscale `E`. Whatever varies across the chart is not a scale defect. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.bestLogScale_singleton","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.bestLogScale_singleton","kind":"theorem","line":304,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L304-L310","doc":"On a one-nuclide sample the best scale is that nuclide's own residual\n`B_i / S_i`: the fitted target ranges over the residual, so it inherits the\nresidual's shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.bestLogScale_eq_mean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.bestLogScale_eq_mean","kind":"theorem","line":284,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L284-L302","doc":"The best log-scale is the sample geometric mean of `B_i/S_i`:\n`logResidual` at `E* = exp(mean ln(B_i/S_i))` is at most its value at any\n`E > 0`. This is exactly the `_geo` the probe uses, so `11.119 MeV` is\n`exp(mean ln(B_i/S_i))` over the 2548 measured nuclides and nothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.logResidual","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.logResidual","kind":"def","line":279,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L279-L282","doc":"The log-least-squares residual of the additive law `B = E·S` at scale\n`E > 0`, over a sample with `B_i, S_i > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.sum_sq_eq_const_add_shape","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.sum_sq_eq_const_add_shape","kind":"theorem","line":270,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L270-L277","doc":"**Constant-plus-shape split of the log-residual.** With `a_i = ln r_i`,\n`Σ a_i² = n·μ² + Σ (a_i − μ)²`: the first term is everything a scale can absorb,\nthe second is what it cannot. This is what the probe's \"shape share\" measures. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.mean_minimises","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.mean_minimises","kind":"theorem","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L263-L268","doc":"The sample mean minimises the sum of squared deviations. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.sum_sq_sub_decomp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.sum_sq_sub_decomp","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L245-L261","doc":"**Sum-of-squares decomposition.** For any `x`,\n`Σ (a_i − x)² = Σ (a_i − μ)² + n·(x − μ)²` with `μ` the sample mean. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.mean","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.mean","kind":"def","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L242-L243","doc":"Sample mean of `a` over `s`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.neg_log_fPP_add_neg_log_fNN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.neg_log_fPP_add_neg_log_fNN","kind":"theorem","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L216-L232","doc":"Over `ℝ` with `0 < q`: the two pure-crossing bond energies sum to\n`2 ln [2]_q`, so `ln [2]_q` is the per-strand coefficient **inside** `S`.\nMultiplying the scale by `[2]_q` (or by `z_D`, or by `1/(q²+1)`) would count a\nfactor the law already carries. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN_eq_inv_sq_div_quantumTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN_eq_inv_sq_div_quantumTwo","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L203-L210","doc":"`b_nn = q⁻² / [2]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP_eq_sq_div_quantumTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP_eq_sq_div_quantumTwo","kind":"theorem","line":197,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L197-L201","doc":"`b_pp = q² / [2]_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP_mul_fNN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP_mul_fNN","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L187-L195","doc":"`b_pp · b_nn = 1/[2]_q²`, identically in `q`. The geometric mean of the two\npure-crossing bonds is the inverse loop value (`qou-xf14`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_eq_E0_mul_tsum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_eq_E0_mul_tsum","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L170-L177","doc":"**The tower-summed scale is the geometric series.** For `1 < q`,\n`E₀^full = E₀ · Σ_{N≥0} ℏ_q^{2N}`: \"brane-tower summed\" means exactly the sum\nof the `ℏ_q^{2N}` ladder, and the ruled `1/(1 − ℏ_q²)` is its value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.hbarQ_sq_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.hbarQ_sq_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L162-L168","doc":"For `1 < q`, `0 ≤ ℏ_q² < 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN_eq_markovZ_sub_heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN_eq_markovZ_sub_heckeH","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L146-L156","doc":"The n–n factor is the Markov parameter minus the skein parameter, `z_D − h`\n— the form `QOU.Hecke.ClosedFormInterfaceFactors.fNN` carries in the\nsquare-root variable. Same object, `q`-variable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_div_E0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_div_E0","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L138-L144","doc":"The ratio of the two derived forms is `q²/(2q−1)` — it does not see the\nanchor `m` at all. At the proton pin this is the `1.00991` of `qou-qkbp`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_closed","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full_closed","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L131-L136","doc":"The tower-summed scale in closed form: `E₀^full = m·q / ((q−1)(2q−1))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.one_sub_hbarQ_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.one_sub_hbarQ_sq","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L124-L129","doc":"`1 − ℏ_q² = (2q − 1)/q²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0_eq_planck","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0_eq_planck","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L117-L122","doc":"`E₀ = m·c_q²·ℏ_q`: the closed form of `rem:energy-scale-planck` is the\nPlanck-unit product, identically in `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0full","kind":"def","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L114-L115","doc":"The tower-summed scale `E₀^full = E₀ / (1 − ℏ_q²)` (owner ruling 2026-09-05). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.E0","kind":"def","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L111-L112","doc":"The single-tick scale `E₀ = m / (q(q−1))`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fNN","kind":"def","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L107-L109","doc":"n–n interface factor `b_nn = 1/(q³+q)` (the closed form of\n`qbeta_bond_factors_derived_probe.py`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.fPP","kind":"def","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L104-L105","doc":"p–p interface factor `b_pp = z_D` (a positive crossing is one Markov reduction). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.heckeH","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L101-L102","doc":"Hecke skein parameter `h = q − q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.markovZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.markovZ","kind":"def","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L98-L99","doc":"Markov parameter, convention D: `z_D = q³/(q²+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.quantumTwo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.quantumTwo","kind":"def","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L95-L96","doc":"Quantum integer `[2]_q = q + q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.hbarQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.hbarQ","kind":"def","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L92-L93","doc":"`ℏ_q = (q−1)/q = 1 − q⁻¹`, the substrate Planck parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.cQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BindingScaleCandidates.cQ","kind":"def","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BindingScaleCandidates.lean#L89-L90","doc":"`c_q = 1/(q−1)`, the substrate speed of information (`rem:energy-scale-planck`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}